# The packages every Mica OS image carries, whatever board it is built for and
# whatever the build declines. Nothing here is selectable: a root with no
# mica-system has no state mounts, no seed and reconcile tools and no operator
# account, and a root with no mica-ca-trust cannot verify a TLS peer.
mica-system
mica-deploy
mica-ca-trust

# The SFTP server dropbear runs (/usr/lib/sftp-server), mica-core's package; every
# product installs it, no board selects it (user decision 2026-09-14).
mica-sftp-server

# The emergency binary, and it is in `common` rather than a feature set on
# purpose. Every other manifest family is declinable -- a feature can be left
# out with --without, a radio with the board that has none -- and a tool an
# operator reaches for when the normal userland is damaged is worth nothing if
# the image in their hands is the build that declined it. It costs one 830 KB
# file, it enables nothing, nothing depends on it, and it changes no PATH; the
# whole of it is /usr/bin/busybox, reached by naming an applet.
mica-busybox

# What every image carried while the Base root held it, and which Base now leaves to the product
# (mica-system-base 20260926-1045 on: the root is the floor, docs/floor-and-options.md). Named here, in common,
# so that every product composes as it did: the SSH server (dropbear through mica-ssh, micad enables it at
# runtime), the console login, the firewall, module loading, the process tools, the device-mapper tools, the time
# zones, the GNU command set and bash. A product that leaves some of them out is the basic variant of
# mica:docs/decisions/2026-09-26-release-variants-full-and-basic.md, not yet made; until it is, nothing declines
# these. mica-ssh and mica-tzdata are packages of the Base pool; the rest are upstream roots of its lock.
mica-ssh
mica-tzdata
login
nftables
kmod
procps
dmsetup
coreutils
findutils
grep
sed
diffutils
gzip
bash
